//@ run-pass
//@ requires: madaros

// D15/D20 -> P-384 plan closeout: this is the REAL Cloudflare 1.1.1.1
// chain, and it now verifies correctly.
//
// History: fixing `x509_verify_signature`'s SHA-384 path (D15) added a
// dispatch into P-256 curve arithmetic. Per RFC 5480 SS2 an
// ecdsa-with-SHA384 signature says nothing about the ISSUER key's curve,
// so at that point in the plan (before P-384 support existed) the
// SHA-384 branch had to reject any issuer key that was not P-256 --
// exactly as the ecdsa-with-SHA256 branch beside it already did. Feeding
// a P-384 key into P-256 math would have been the classic confusion bug:
// the wrong group order and the wrong field prime, producing a verdict
// that means nothing. This file originally pinned that fail-closed
// rejection as the correct interim behaviour.
//
// The certificates pinned below are REAL, fetched live on 2026-08-27
// with:
//   openssl s_client -connect 1.1.1.1:443 -servername cloudflare-dns.com \
//     -showcerts </dev/null
//
//   leaf:         CN=cloudflare-dns.com
//                 key P-256, Signature Algorithm: ecdsa-with-SHA384
//   intermediate: CN=SSL.com SSL Intermediate CA ECC R2  (the issuer)
//                 key **P-384**, Signature Algorithm: ecdsa-with-SHA384
//
// So Cloudflare's own 1.1.1.1 chain is ecdsa-with-SHA384 signed by a
// P-384 key. The D15 fix (P-256 issuer + SHA-384) is real and separately
// proven in x509_chain_ecdsa_sha384_signature.sio, but on its own it did
// NOT unblock this chain: `stdlib/crypto/ecdsa_p256.sio` implements P-256
// only, so the algorithm guard correctly refused to run a P-384 key
// through P-256 arithmetic.
//
// UPDATE (2026-08-27, madaros-ecdsa-p384-design plan): full P-384 support
// has since landed -- curve constants and verifier
// (`stdlib/crypto/ecdsa_p384.sio`), the secp384r1 OID
// (`stdlib/x509/oid.sio`), `PUBKEY_ALG_EC_P384` public-key recognition
// (`stdlib/x509/cert.sio`), and dispatch by issuer key algorithm in
// `x509_verify_signature`. See
// docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-27-madaros-ecdsa-p384-design.md. This
// exact real intermediate now correctly classifies as
// `PUBKEY_ALG_EC_P384` (not `PUBKEY_ALG_UNKNOWN`), and the leaf's
// ecdsa-with-SHA384 signature now correctly VERIFIES against it -- proven
// below against the live-fetched Cloudflare bytes themselves, the single
// most valuable fixture in this plan (this is the literal motivating
// case, not a synthetic stand-in). The synthetic, openssl-independent
// analogue of this same P-256-leaf/P-384-issuer shape is pinned in
// `tests/run-pass/x509_chain_ecdsa_p384_signature.sio`; the adversarial
// per-curve rejection case (this same leaf against an UNRELATED P-256
// root) is pinned in
// `tests/run-pass/x509_chain_ecdsa_p384_cross_curve_rejected.sio`.
//
// Note this asserts nothing about validity dates -- only signature
// verification -- so these fixtures do not rot when the certificates expire.

